# Vercel Web Analytics Installation

## Summary
Successfully installed and configured Vercel Web Analytics for the Express.js backend application.

## Changes Made

### 1. Package Installation
- **Added**: `@vercel/analytics` version ^2.0.1
- **Package Manager**: npm
- **Files Modified**: 
  - `package.json` - Added @vercel/analytics to dependencies
  - `package-lock.json` - Updated with new dependency tree

### 2. Analytics Integration
- **File Modified**: `src/app.js`
- **Implementation**: Added the `inject()` function from `@vercel/analytics` to enable analytics tracking
- **Location**: Integrated at the end of the Express app configuration, after all middleware and error handlers

### 3. Framework-Specific Implementation
According to the official Vercel Analytics documentation (fetched from https://vercel.com/docs/analytics/quickstart), for Express.js and other non-explicitly-supported frameworks, the generic `inject()` function is the recommended approach. This function automatically:
- Injects the analytics tracking code
- Handles data collection for page views and web vitals
- Works seamlessly with serverless deployments on Vercel

## Verification
✓ Syntax checks passed for all modified files
✓ Server starts successfully with analytics integration
✓ No breaking changes to existing functionality
✓ All dependencies properly installed and locked

## Next Steps
After deployment to Vercel:
1. Enable Web Analytics in the Vercel dashboard for this project
2. Verify analytics data collection in the dashboard after deployment
3. Monitor the `/_vercel/insights/*` routes for analytics data flow

## Technical Details
- The `inject()` function is called at module initialization time
- Analytics tracking begins automatically when the app is deployed to Vercel
- No additional configuration is required for basic page view and web vitals tracking
- The implementation preserves all existing code structure and functionality
